over the weekend the international  community came together and started a  sanction russia many of its citizens and  the russian central bank this is  unprecedented type action with a global  coordination of many many countries what  we see here is countries like italy and  hungary now coming together to support  russia's expulsion from the swift  banking system then we saw boris johnson  come out and say very explicitly they've  taken decisive action and they're  working with their international  partners including an important first  step of ejecting russian banks from  swift they will keep working together to  ensure putin pays for the price of his  aggression and then of course the united  states what we have here is secretary  antony blinken who said as a result of  putin's war on ukraine we join the  leaders of the eu france germany italy  uk and canada to ensure key sanctioned  russian banks are disconnected from  swift now this stuff sounds really big  really bad what you have to remember is  that the customers of those russian  banks those are actual individual  citizens people who probably actually  don't want to be in war with ukraine if  we go ahead and we take a look here what  we can see is that russia's money is not  sitting in the united states for the  most part only eight and a half percent  or so of their foreign exchange reserves  sits within the u.s or u.s banking now  china china's got 17.7 percent that's  still accessible to russia there are no  sanctions that can touch that chinese  held money but places like france japan  germany us the uk and others all  sanctioning the russian assets not  allowing them to leave their country  borders and so what is the impact of  this on the russian ruble the currency  of russia well of course it is down it  is down significantly and we're seeing  it continuing to trade down today the  russian ruble is in a free fall now  sitting at about one penny per ruble  down significantly since the start of  this invasion this is the impact of  economic sanctions this is what the  international community is doing they're  tanking the currency and they're  destroying the economy trying to apply  enough pressure to get vladimir putin to  call off the war it's important to  understand though that economic  sanctions don't actually hurt the leader  of a country instead the goal of the  sanctions is to inflict maximum pain on  the everyday citizens with the hopes  that they'll get so fed up that they'll  be in so much pain they'll rise up and  they'll either force their leaders to  make different decisions or they'll  actually remove those leaders from their  leadership position well here we see  that people in russia literally saying  my hotel asked me to settle the bill  early because they're not sure if credit  cards are going to work once the  sanctions kick in and then we've got  this photo of people lined up for hours  standing outside of atms trying to get a  sliver of their wealth back so that if  they want to leave russia or if they  simply want to buy goods and services  they aren't going to bear the brunt of  such economic sanctions ultimately  sanctions are financial censorship and  whether you agree with them or you  disagree with them it is very clear that  the international community is going to  continue to pursue these types of  activities whenever somebody steps out  of line or does something that they  disagree with and so ultimately what the  world is watching  is that a decentralized digital  censorship resistant technology is gonna  become even more important in the future
 

